  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1137 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nSunday night into early Monday continues to appear more active\nthan the current night. Low shower and thunderstorm chances will\nhang on during the evening hours across parts of far northeast\nOklahoma into northwest Arkansas, but much greater coverage\nremains likely after midnight into the daylight hours Monday\nmorning. A disturbance will move through the area on the eastern\nperiphery of the upper level high centered to the west, with\ndevelopment beginning across parts of northeast Oklahoma before\nextending southeastward across Arkansas before dawn Monday. Some\nof the recent CAMs keep isolated to shower potential farther west\nin the area into Monday afternoon. Will not adjust the NBM POPs\nfor this just yet but the trend will need to be monitored and\npotentially adjusted for. Assuming this westward and delayed trend\ndoes not occur, high temperatures across southeast Oklahoma will\npush 105 in some locations Monday afternoon. A front will move \nthrough the area during the day Tuesday, with its progression \ndetermining where the hotter temperatures setup and how cool \nnorthern parts of the area get. With current expectations, areas \nsouth of I-40 will again push 105, with highs across the northern \nhalf of the area /only/ in the mid and upper 90s. Heat headlines\nwill be needed in parts of the area both Monday and Tuesday. \n\nFrom mid to late in the upcoming week, the basic weather pattern\nremains similar, with the upper level ridge centered to the west\nleading to periods of low shower and thunderstorm chances for some\nand a corresponding brief period of cooler temperatures.\nTemperatures on average should be above normal, save for the lucky\nlocations and days with rain and residual cloud cover during the \nday.",
